  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🦅 Imperial Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Latin and Italian-style names for the people of Cyrodiil, with a Legion rank if you want one."
    },
    "intro": "Imperials come from Cyrodiil, the heart of the Empire, and their names sound like Rome with an Italian accent: General Tullius, commanding the Legion in Skyrim, sits alongside Vittoria Vici and Adrianne Avenicci. This **Imperial name generator** makes new names in that style, a first name and surname, a single name or a name with a Legion rank, for Skyrim, Elder Scrolls Online or your own story. It's a fan-made, unofficial tool, not connected to Bethesda Softworks or ZeniMax, and it never gives you the name of a character from the games.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How Imperial names work",
        "content": "<p>Most Imperials in the games have a first name and a surname, and they come in two flavours:</p><ul><li><strong>Latin</strong>, like a Roman citizen: <strong>General Tullius</strong>, and <strong>Proventus Avenicci</strong>, the steward of Whiterun.</li><li><strong>Italian</strong>, as if the Empire had moved on a few centuries: <strong>Adrianne Avenicci</strong> at Warmaiden's in Whiterun, <strong>Carlotta Valentia</strong> on its market stalls, and <strong>Adelaisa Vendicci</strong> of the East Empire Company in Solitude.</li></ul><p>The two flavours mix freely, even in one family: Proventus's daughter is Adrianne. This generator does the same, so you might get a Latin first name with an Italian surname, like <em>Placidus Castellani</em>, or the other way round.</p><p>Imperials in the games rarely use the three-part Roman name of praenomen, nomen and cognomen. For real Romans built that way, try the <a href=\"roman-name-generator.html\">Roman Name Generator</a>.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Eight good Imperial names",
